From b44fb77cfd838c323198dd3d703c956e424018b7 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ian Blenke <ian@blenke.com>
Date: Fri, 31 Jul 2026 22:58:12 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 08079/11362] [outer-loop] Correct the split-sweep amendment:
 VRAM is measured, tok/s is unknown

The amendment committed in bae01b89df said arms 1-2 "will not reproduce" the
2026-07-28 targets, full stop. That is right for one half and unsupported for
the other, and the difference changes what a reader does with the result.

VRAM: settled. QAT UD-Q4_K_XL is 16.10 GiB vs Q4_K_M's 17.07 GiB (993 MiB), and
the QAT head-to-head independently recorded residency_mib_at_cell_start = 20430
against arm 1's pinned 21416 (986 MiB lower). Two independent figures agreeing
to ~7 MiB. Measured, not predicted -- and it is exactly the ~1 GB the switch was
made to save.

Throughput: unknown. No QAT decode rate has ever been measured here. The
head-to-head captured only per-cell elapsed_s (~300 s), which is dominated by
induction and carries no tok/s signal. Arm 1 could land inside +-10% of 36.07.

Why this is worth a commit rather than a silent edit: under the old wording, a
reader who ran arm 1 and got ~35 tok/s would file it as an expected mismatch and
ignore it. It is the opposite -- a near-reproduction is evidence the single-GPU
baseline is sound and the split arms can be trusted against it, and it is the
only cross-session check this sweep has.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>
